# This directory is the mod, and only the mod. The game lives outside it, reached through the
# `game-windows` / `game-linux` symlinks that `code/deploy.py` patches (CHANGELOG §62).
#
# It defaults to ignoring EVERYTHING and opts individual paths back in. That inversion is
# deliberate: what is untracked here is third-party (the BepInEx runtime and the Edi application,
# both fetched from pinned, checksummed upstream builds), not redistributable (the game itself,
# now only reachable through the symlinks), or regenerable (the release zip, the capture videos).
# A denylist would silently pick up the first new one that appeared - and the symlinks make that
# a live risk, since a careless rule would follow one into a 2 GB game install.
#
# No binary is tracked, including our own three plugin DLLs. They are build output: one
# `dotnet build code/edimod/PncEdi.csproj -c Release` produces all three. Building needs a game
# install to compile against (every plugin references the game's own Assembly-CSharp.dll), so a
# clone is a source tree rather than a runnable install - README.md says so, and players get the
# built DLLs from the release archive instead.
#
# Note the allowlist: a new top-level directory is invisible to git until it is opted in here.
# `learnings/` was, for exactly as long as it took to notice.

/*
!/.gitignore
!/.gitattributes
!/*.md
# TODO.md is private working notes, not a document about the mod. It is one person's handoff to
# their next session - half-formed diagnoses, what is annoying, what is not worth doing - and it
# is the one file here written with no reader in mind. Untracked so it can stay that way: a
# public TODO is either curated (which costs the honesty that makes it useful) or it is a
# to-do list published to strangers. The documents that reference it now check whether one
# exists rather than assuming it does.
/TODO.md
!/learnings/
!/code/
!/BepInEx/
!/Edi/

# --- code: sources and tools, never build output or downloads -----------------------------
code/**/bin/
code/**/obj/
code/**/.vs/
code/__pycache__/
code/dist/build/
code/dist/cache/
code/dist/bepinex/

# --- BepInEx: our config and our plugin, and nothing of the runtime -----------------------
#
# The runtime is no longer here at all - it is deployed into the game directories from the
# pinned pack. These two files are the whole of the mod's presence in a BepInEx tree.
BepInEx/*
!BepInEx/config/
BepInEx/config/*
!BepInEx/config/com.edi.pnc.cfg
# plugins/ holds only build output, so nothing here is named back in. `deploy.py` writes the
# three DLLs into it on every build and both game installs are patched from there.
!BepInEx/plugins/
BepInEx/plugins/*

# --- custom-enemy packages: the text, not the media ---------------------------------------
#
# A package is data - a manifest, PNG sheets, funscripts and (for the witch) H.264/VP8 video.
# The two real packages are 53 MB of that, almost all of it media, and media does not belong in
# a source repo: git history is permanent, so a clone carries it forever and a takedown means
# rewriting history rather than deleting a file. The media reaches players instead as its own
# archive - `release.py --package <name>` - posted beside the release.
#
# What does belong here is the text, which is ~1 MB and three different things:
#
#   - the funscripts, which are **ours**. They were authored and measured by this project the
#     same way Edi/Gallery/detailed/ was, and no third-party licence touches them. They used to
#     be discarded by a blanket ignore aimed at the video beside them.
#   - the manifests, which are mostly game-side tuning - maxHealth, detectionRange, pivot,
#     scale, spawnWeight, captureDistance. Untracked, a tuning pass lived in the changelog and
#     in two game installs and in no tracked file (§134 moved the witch's captureDistance and
#     that is exactly what happened to it).
#   - SOURCE.txt, which credits the artists and so is the one file with the least business
#     existing only on one machine.
#
# The rule below is deny-by-default on purpose: everything under a package is ignored, and the
# three kinds of text above are named back in. A new media format someone adds later is
# therefore ignored without anyone having to remember to ignore it.
#
# A fresh clone gets a package's text with no media beside it, and the package will not load
# until the media is restored from its archive. BepInEx/custom-enemies/CUSTOM-ENEMIES.md says so.
#
# `code/deploy.py` copies whatever packages are in the working tree into both game installs, so
# dropping an untracked package in here and deploying still works.
!BepInEx/custom-enemies/
BepInEx/custom-enemies/*
!BepInEx/custom-enemies/*.md
!BepInEx/custom-enemies/_example/
# Descend into a package (git cannot re-include below an excluded directory), ignore its
# contents, then name back the text.
!BepInEx/custom-enemies/*/
BepInEx/custom-enemies/*/**
!BepInEx/custom-enemies/*/**/
!BepInEx/custom-enemies/*/*.json
!BepInEx/custom-enemies/*/SOURCE.txt
!BepInEx/custom-enemies/*/funscripts/**/*.funscript

# --- Edi: the gallery and the source scripts, not the Edi application ---------------------
#
# EdiConfig.json is excluded ON PURPOSE and must stay excluded: its device names embed
# per-user Handy connection keys ("The Handy [xxxxxxxx]"), which are effectively credentials
# for controlling that hardware. It is also per-user by design (see PROJECT.md), so there is
# nothing to gain by tracking it.
Edi/*
!Edi/Gallery/
!Edi/_reference/
Edi/_reference/*
!Edi/_reference/source-scripts/

# --- logs, scratch and release output -----------------------------------------------------
#
# /dist is where release.py writes the zip. It is reproducible from any commit, so it is
# output, not source.
*.log
*.bak
